\name{BaseRQqlineLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRQqlineLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Q-Q Reference Line Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{qqline()} as the reference line it draws.

\code{qqnorm()} and \code{qqplot()} became readable in #251, and \code{qqline()} -- how
nearly every Q-Q plot in the wild is finished -- was listed in \code{LOW} with
no reading at all, purely so a chart carrying one would decline rather
than come out as a scatter with a drawn mark silently missing from it.
That was the lower of the two claims; this is the reading (#252).
\subsection{Why it is recorded at all}{

\code{stats::qqline()} ends in \code{abline(int, slope, ...)}, and that call is
reached from \emph{inside} the \code{stats} namespace, where maidr's search-path
wrapper never sees it. Measured: before \code{qqline} was listed, \verb{qqnorm(x); qqline(x)} recorded exactly one call and the reference line left no trace.
}

\subsection{Where the endpoints come from}{

Not from the drawn grob and not re-derived. \code{stats::qqline}'s body is four
lines, and the line it draws is the one through two points:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{y <- quantile(y, probs, names = FALSE, type = qtype, na.rm = TRUE)
x <- distribution(probs)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

with \code{probs} defaulting to \code{c(0.25, 0.75)} and \code{distribution} to \code{qnorm}.
This class asks \code{stats} for the same two anchors, from the \strong{call's own}
arguments, so a \code{qqline()} written with a non-default \code{probs}, \code{qtype} or
\code{distribution} is read from what it was given rather than from the
defaults. \code{datax = TRUE} swaps which of the two the slope is taken over,
and \code{qqline()} takes its own copy of that argument rather than inheriting
the plot's -- so a \code{qqline(datax = TRUE)} over a \code{qqnorm(datax = FALSE)}
is expressible, and is read from the \code{qqline} call.
}

\subsection{Why the x range is not the parent's}{

\code{BaseRLineLayerProcessor$extract_abline_data()} takes its x range from
\code{get_x_range_from_group()}, which reads the group's HIGH call's first
argument as the x data. On a \code{qqnorm} group that argument is the
\strong{sample}, not the theoretical quantiles the chart puts on x -- so
inheriting it would stretch the line across the wrong interval, which is
the class of mistake the Q-Q reading exists to avoid. The range comes from
the drawn pairs instead, which \code{BaseRQqLayerProcessor} already
computes from \code{stats}' own output.

Highlighting needs nothing new: \verb{qqnorm(x); qqline(x)} and
\verb{plot(x, y); abline(0, 1)} export the \emph{same} grob,
\code{graphics-plot-1-abline-ab-1}, so the parent's abline selector reaches it
unchanged.
}
}
